WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.197
A container image is assembled from build instructions and filesystem layers.

00:00:05.197 --> 00:00:12.057
The base image may provide an operating system, a minimal distribution, or a language-specific runtime.

00:00:12.057 --> 00:00:16.423
Later layers add packages, application dependencies, configuration, and code.

00:00:16.423 --> 00:00:19.541
Patch scope begins with those inputs.

00:00:19.541 --> 00:00:23.491
Operating-system packages may be inherited from the base.

00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:28.272
Language runtimes and package managers introduce their own release cycles.

00:00:28.272 --> 00:00:34.716
Application libraries can be installed directly, pulled transitively, or copied from another build stage.

00:00:34.716 --> 00:00:39.497
The container build file defines how the artifact is created.

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:47.189
Pinning, repository configuration, build stages, copied files, and cleanup all affect what remains in the final image.

00:00:47.189 --> 00:00:55.296
Updating a package in one layer does not help if an older vulnerable copy is still present elsewhere.

00:00:55.296 --> 00:00:57.583
Shared ancestry creates fan-out.

00:00:57.583 --> 00:01:08.185
Many teams may build from the same organization base image or dependency set, so one vulnerable component can appear in dozens of application images.

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:14.629
A base-image fix reduces risk only after dependent images are rebuilt, promoted, and redeployed.

00:01:14.629 --> 00:01:18.995
Tags can move, while digests identify exact image content.

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:27.518
Patch records should connect source revision, base digest, dependency inventory, build provenance, image digest, registry location, and deployed workload.

00:01:27.518 --> 00:01:32.299
This chain lets teams prove which version is actually running.

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:38.744
Minimize image contents and maintain a software bill of materials to make scope visible.

00:01:38.744 --> 00:01:44.356
Removing unused tools and packages reduces both attack surface and remediation workload.

00:01:44.356 --> 00:01:53.295
The objective is not simply a newer tag; it is a reproducible image whose vulnerable components were replaced or removed.
